WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.721 --> 00:00:06.703
Can you imagine traveling on the same train for seven days?

2
00:00:07.563 --> 00:00:11.205
If you take the Trans-Siberian Railroad, that is exactly what you will do.

3
00:00:12.065 --> 00:00:17.487
You will pass through eight time zones and speed past two continents, as well as the world's largest

4
00:00:17.547 --> 00:00:21.909
lake, traveling from the heart of Europe until you finally reach the Pacific Ocean.

5
00:00:22.829 --> 00:00:27.911
The Trans-Siberian Railway is the longest railway system in the world, stretching over 9,200 kilometers.

6
00:00:29.768 --> 00:00:33.470
It begins in Moscow and extends eastward over the Ural Mountains.

7
00:00:34.651 --> 00:00:41.175
It continues through Siberia, an area which constitutes almost all of northern Asia, to its final destination, the

8
00:00:41.215 --> 00:00:42.615
port town of Vladivostok.

9
00:00:44.056 --> 00:00:52.321
Before the Trans-Siberian Railway was built, traveling from western Russia to Vladivostok took weeks—sometimes even months—due to Siberia's

10
00:00:52.361 --> 00:00:53.081
harsh climate.

11
00:00:54.102 --> 00:00:59.136
In the extreme north of Siberia, ice and snow can cover the land for up to eleven months

12
00:00:59.176 --> 00:00:59.677
of the year.

13
00:01:00.697 --> 00:01:06.698
Weather conditions in the southern part of Siberia, however, are much more hospitable, with average winter temperatures of

14
00:01:06.758 --> 00:01:11.680
about -20°C and summer temperatures of 20°C.

15
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:17.461
This is one of the reasons Russian engineers decided to build the railway in the south; it could

16
00:01:17.481 --> 00:01:18.782
be utilized year-round.

17
00:01:19.682 --> 00:01:22.743
Construction of the Trans-Siberian train route began in 1891 and lasted 25 years.

18
00:01:22.763 --> 00:01:31.960
During those years thousands of prisoners, soldiers, and peasants worked through frigid winters and muggy summers.

19
00:01:32.821 --> 00:01:37.825
They began laying track at opposite ends of the country, and the railway was completed when the tracks

20
00:01:37.885 --> 00:01:39.947
met in the middle in 1916.

21
00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:47.413
More routes have been added over time, and today the Trans-Siberian Railway connects Russia with China and Mongolia

22
00:01:47.513 --> 00:01:47.933
as well.

23
00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:54.739
The Trans-Siberian Railway has become an essential part of the Russian transportation system for several reasons.

24
00:01:55.599 --> 00:02:00.644
For one, it is used to transport 30 percent of the country's exports to the east coast of

25
00:02:00.744 --> 00:02:02.746
Russia or to its western borders.

26
00:02:03.606 --> 00:02:10.112
These natural resources and manufactured goods are then sent on to North America via the Pacific Ocean or

27
00:02:10.192 --> 00:02:10.973
to Western Europe.

28
00:02:11.914 --> 00:02:14.116
But ordinary people use the railroad as well.

29
00:02:14.837 --> 00:02:17.179
It is used by Russians to travel within their country.

30
00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:24.029
It also attracts international tourists who want the unique adventure of traversing all of Asia, watching an entire

31
00:02:24.109 --> 00:02:26.510
continent pass by outside their train windows.

32
00:02:27.331 --> 00:02:29.773
Let's take a trip from Moscow to Vladivostok.

33
00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:35.036
Along the way we'll experience the wonders, sights, and sounds of the world's longest train journey.

34
00:02:35.957 --> 00:02:40.259
Traveling on the Trans-Siberian Railway is a great way to meet people from different cultures.

35
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:45.023
Some travel for short distances, some for the entire seven days.

36
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:48.845
For one week the train becomes everyone's shared home.

37
00:02:49.646 --> 00:02:56.305
So passengers quickly become friends, sharing food and drinks, telling stories, singing songs, and playing games to pass

38
00:02:56.365 --> 00:02:56.866
the time.

39
00:02:58.086 --> 00:03:00.727
There are many trains that use the Trans-Siberian Railroad.

40
00:03:01.488 --> 00:03:04.669
The accommodations available depend on the train you choose.

41
00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:08.951
You can travel in a first-class, second-class, or third-class car.

42
00:03:09.871 --> 00:03:15.634
The first-class cars have cabins for two people, while cabins in second class contain simple beds for four

43
00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:21.256
passengers. Some trains also have third-class cars, which are communal spaces with many bunks.

44
00:03:22.112 --> 00:03:27.256
These cars can get kind of rowdy, especially if a group of soldiers decides to have a party

45
00:03:27.296 --> 00:03:29.477
and spend the night playing their guitars and singing.

46
00:03:30.398 --> 00:03:34.280
Every car on every train has two bathrooms, one at each end of the car.

47
00:03:35.101 --> 00:03:39.964
Food is available from a restaurant car, although most people bring snacks with them or buy them on

48
00:03:40.004 --> 00:03:40.685
the platforms.

49
00:03:41.565 --> 00:03:46.568
There is an endless supply of hot water from a tank called a samovar, so many people just

50
00:03:46.628 --> 00:03:47.209
make noodles.

51
00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.652
Each car of the train, regardless of class, has an attendant.

52
00:03:52.416 --> 00:03:57.400
These people are responsible for the comfort and safety of the travelers in their car, so they are

53
00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.201
very important.

54
00:03:58.881 --> 00:04:03.124
They ensure that people get off at the right stops and make sure passengers don't stay out on

55
00:04:03.144 --> 00:04:04.485
the platforms for too long.

56
00:04:05.326 --> 00:04:12.311
Without these attendants, passengers too busy buying local delicacies and souvenirs from vendors might not notice the train

57
00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:13.852
leaving and be left behind!

58
00:04:14.853 --> 00:04:18.396
On some trains the attendants are also the people to order some tea from.

59
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.043
Otherwise you'll have to get some hot water from the samovar and make it yourself.

60
00:04:24.405 --> 00:04:27.407
The first part of the journey takes us through European Russia.

61
00:04:28.228 --> 00:04:30.510
The rural landscape in this area is picturesque.

62
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:37.696
The train passes many small villages where you can see Russian Orthodox churches with their onion-shaped cupolas, and

63
00:04:37.776 --> 00:04:39.377
fields with local farm workers.

64
00:04:40.138 --> 00:04:44.662
Before long the train travels into the Ural Mountains, which separate Europe and Asia.

65
00:04:45.562 --> 00:04:49.501
A white obelisk along the route marks the divide between the two continents.

66
00:04:50.361 --> 00:04:54.702
After a day of traveling, we reach the first major stop: Yekaterinburg.

67
00:04:55.282 --> 00:05:01.564
This beautiful city is almost 1,700 kilometers from Moscow and is the capital of the Ural region.

68
00:05:02.284 --> 00:05:07.926
This is also where Russia's imperial family, the Romanovs, was murdered after the Russian Revolution.

69
00:05:08.766 --> 00:05:13.807
There is a memorial to the Romanovs here, as well as many other places to visit, so tourists

70
00:05:13.927 --> 00:05:17.637
often get off at Yekaterinburg and stay a few days to sightsee.

71
00:05:18.798 --> 00:05:23.382
From Yekaterinburg we leave the Ural Mountains and continue into continental Siberia.

72
00:05:24.103 --> 00:05:29.187
For the next five days, the train will travel across the taiga and the steppe, which make up

73
00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:30.788
most of the Siberian landscape.

74
00:05:31.809 --> 00:05:35.312
Although there are a few stops, the land is largely unpopulated.

75
00:05:36.252 --> 00:05:41.376
Staring out the window of the train at the seemingly endless terrain, you can't help being impressed by

76
00:05:41.416 --> 00:05:42.297
its stark beauty.

77
00:05:43.218 --> 00:05:49.283
After two days the train stops at Novosibirsk, the third largest city in Russia after Moscow and St.

78
00:05:49.323 --> 00:05:55.468
Petersburg. It was founded on the banks of the Ob River when work on the Trans-Siberian Railway started.

79
00:05:56.328 --> 00:06:01.933
Now it is home to opera and ballet companies, theaters, museums, art galleries, and one of Russia's best

80
00:06:02.013 --> 00:06:08.158
universities. The next major town on the journey is Irkutsk, which is where you'd disembark to see Lake

81
00:06:08.198 --> 00:06:14.323
Baikal. Lake Baikal is the largest and deepest lake in the world, containing 20 percent of the world's

82
00:06:14.343 --> 00:06:15.684
supply of fresh water.

83
00:06:16.636 --> 00:06:20.978
Its crystal-clear waters, wildlife, and breathtaking scenery are well worth seeing.

84
00:06:21.958 --> 00:06:26.740
The lake was too big to lay track around when the railroad was first being built, so for

85
00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:32.363
many years, the passengers of the Trans-Siberian had to take a ferry across the lake and switch to

86
00:06:32.423 --> 00:06:34.123
another train on the opposite side.

87
00:06:35.024 --> 00:06:38.785
In the winters sleds carried the passengers across the lake's frozen surface.

88
00:06:39.766 --> 00:06:45.228
Later a series of bridges and tunnels were built, and now the tracks curve around Lake Baikal's southern

89
00:06:45.268 --> 00:06:52.681
tip. After three and a half days and about 5,600 kilometers, we reach the town of Ulan-Ude.

90
00:06:53.581 --> 00:06:57.725
This town is very close to Mongolia and is in the heart of Asian Russia.

91
00:06:58.646 --> 00:07:00.887
Here the train route splits into three lines.

92
00:07:01.648 --> 00:07:07.233
Trains heading to Mongolia and China begin their journey here, while the main route continues on to Vladivostok.

93
00:07:08.113 --> 00:07:11.456
Trains heading to China, however, cannot simply cross the Chinese border.

94
00:07:12.337 --> 00:07:17.425
Russia and Mongolia both use a wider gauge of railway track than China, so the trains have to

95
00:07:17.465 --> 00:07:21.487
have the lower part of their carriages changed, which can take several hours.

96
00:07:22.828 --> 00:07:28.912
The last major stop before Vladivostok is Khabarovsk, which is 8,000 kilometers from Moscow.

97
00:07:29.713 --> 00:07:32.795
From here we finally make our way to the last stop of the line.

98
00:07:33.695 --> 00:07:40.399
After seven days and more than 9,200 kilometers, the train pulls into Vladivostok, Russia's biggest port on the

99
00:07:40.439 --> 00:07:41.180
Pacific Ocean.

100
00:07:42.248 --> 00:07:48.092
At the train terminal, a monument marks the last kilometer of the Trans-Siberian Railway, with a total length

101
00:07:48.132 --> 00:07:50.133
of 9,288 kilometers.

102
00:07:51.073 --> 00:07:56.897
Until 1991 foreigners were forbidden to visit this city because it was home to Russia's Pacific Naval Fleet.

103
00:07:57.957 --> 00:08:02.780
Even Russians who did not have the proper security clearance needed special permission to enter the city.

104
00:08:03.601 --> 00:08:08.784
Now you can join the many tourists who get their picture taken beside the monument to commemorate their

105
00:08:08.804 --> 00:08:16.079
journey. The Trans-Siberian Railway not only connects Europe and Asia, it also connects the many cultures along its

106
00:08:16.119 --> 00:08:22.501
route. Traveling its thousands of kilometers is the best way to fully experience the beauty and expanse of

107
00:08:22.561 --> 00:08:22.902
Russia.
